From e191cca3f03cedb3a3447101e99184dd3f707c81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 04:52:49 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] 360 viewer: draw a cube at each tracked pose again

Restores the original hello_xr sample's per-pose cubes, which this
player dropped when it replaced the geometry pipeline wholesale with the
fullscreen-triangle 360 path. The cubes vector has been arriving in
RenderView fully populated and being discarded ever since.

Adds a second graphics pipeline with its own vertex/fragment pair, drawn
after the 360 background with depth test. It shares the existing
VkPipelineLayout: the push-constant block is declared identically and
only the leading mvp is read, so no second layout is needed. The cube
geometry and its buffers were already being uploaded -- the skybox uses
the same vertex buffer. compile_glsl derives the shader stage from the
file name and so allows only one file per stage; the new pair gets
explicit rules, the same way yuv_frag.glsl already does.

Two things had to be fixed before anything was visible on hardware:

The 360 background wrote gl_Position.z = 0.0, the NEAREST depth, with
depth writes enabled. It filled the depth buffer with the minimum value
and every cube drawn afterwards failed the LESS test and was silently
discarded. A background belongs at the far plane; it is now 0.9999, just
under the 1.0 the depth buffer is cleared to, since exactly 1.0 would
fail against the clear value and the background itself would stop
drawing.

The sample also draws a 25 cm cube per reference space, and in a 3dof
session the head sits exactly at the Local origin -- so that cube lands
centred on the viewer's face, and with cullMode NONE its inner faces
render too, sealing the viewer inside an opaque box that hides
everything else. Those cubes are now off by default behind
HELLO_XR_SPACE_CUBES=1.

Verified on hardware: controller cubes visible, and they stay fixed in
space while the head rotates around them.

Known limitation: the 360 background has its own recenter (a yaw offset
applied to its pose) and the cubes use the runtime pose directly, so a
recenter rotates the background but not the cubes.
